WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:01.800
All right everyone.

2
00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:05.520
Hello and welcome to the opening
keynote session

3
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.960
for the Electronic Literature Conference
2026.

4
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:12.040
Unsupervised.

5
00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:13.840
Thank you all very much for being here.

6
00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.720
And greetings to those of you
who are joining by watching

7
00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:21.480
the recording in the Stars repository
after this event.

8
00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:25.760
We are really glad that you all are here, 
and we especially want to

9
00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.720
welcome Liliana von Bertram,
who's our presenter for today.

10
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.680
My name is Peter Edgar and I'm
the logistics chair for this conference.

11
00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:38.680
And I'm going to introduce Liliana Von.

12
00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.680
And with this bio.

13
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.320
William von Bertram is an African American
writer, poet, artist and educator

14
00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:50.120
working at the intersection
of computation, AI, race, and gender.

15
00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.320
Their practice engages
foundational questions

16
00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.880
in contemporary poetics
and AI studies how algorithmic systems

17
00:00:55.880 --> 00:01:00.000
encode and reproduce racial and gender
bias, how machine generated texts

18
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.120
troubles authorship and literary value,
and what critical and esthetic methods

19
00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:07.960
might constitute a politics of resistance
within computational form.

20
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.680
They're the author of Travesty Generator
by Noemi Press,

21
00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:16.120
a book of computational poetry longlisted
for the National Book Award in Poetry,

22
00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:21.000
and which also won the Anna Rabinowitz
Prize and the Poetry Society of America.

23
00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:25.720
Their other poetry collections
include Negative Money, Personal Silence,

24
00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:29.040
How Minor, How Narrow, My
Escapes, A Slice from the Cake,

25
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:32.160
Made of Air,
and A Storm Is Blowing from Paradise.

26
00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:36.320
Their chapbook A Black Story may contain
sensitive content, which I have here

27
00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:39.360
with me, written in collaboration
with the large language model,

28
00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.840
won the 2023 Diagram New Michigan Press
Chapbook Contest, and they're the coeditor

29
00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.960
with Nick Montfort of Output and Anthology

30
00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.760
of Computer Generated Texts, 1953 to 2023.

31
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:54.040
Bertram is a recipient
of the National Endowment for Arts Poetry

32
00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:58.040
Fellowship, the 2024 Foundation
for Contemporary Arts Poetry Grant,

33
00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.120
and the 2024 Deutsch Foundation
Rubies Grant.

34
00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:03.040
They currently direct the MFA in Creative

35
00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:06.280
Writing at the University of Maryland,
and serve as an affiliate faculty

36
00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:09.640
in the Artificial Intelligence
Interdisciplinary Institute at Maryland.

37
00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:12.680
And I'll say one more
that they edited this

38
00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.040
Michigan Quarterly Review
special issue featuring many of you.

39
00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:20.400
And I maintain a low grade
parasocial relationship

40
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.440
with the possum
that they collect pictures of.

41
00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:25.440
So thank you so much.

42
00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.000
Please
welcome warmly, Lillian von Bertram.

43
00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.000
Oh, thank you so much.

44
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.160
That was wonderful.

45
00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.000
Yes.

46
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:34.960
The possum.

47
00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:36.280
We can talk about that another time.

48
00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:38.960
But there is a possum, among other things.

49
00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.120
Thank you so much, Petey.

50
00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:44.440
Anastasia, everyone, for being here 
and for inviting me.

51
00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:47.760
I think ten years, I was like,
what is yellow?

52
00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:48.920
How can I get there?

53
00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:52.120
And would not have imagined
that I would one day be giving a keynote.

54
00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:55.320
So I'm just really pleased
and humbled to be talking to

55
00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:58.320
so many artists
and thinkers that I deeply admire.

56
00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.800
I've taken the title of my talk
from Raymond

57
00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:04.960
Roussel, who wrote a book called
how I Wrote Certain of My Books.

58
00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:07.160
And this nod to
Roussel will become clear later.

59
00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:08.760
But don't take it to mean that

60
00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.640
I in any way feel comparable to her cell
or his level of eccentricity,

61
00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:15.920
which I have yet to remotely approach,
and I think that's a good thing.

62
00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:20.840
Our overlaps are few, but real methods
of constraint or procedure in writing,

63
00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:24.240
and I find his title fitting
because I'm going to do some explanation

64
00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.760
in response to a question
I was recently asked.

65
00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:30.880
And to answer this question
does require some trips down memory lane

66
00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:31.760
by memory lane.

67
00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:36.440
And so I think you in advance 
for your indulgence, some storytelling.

68
00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:41.680
Recently,

69
00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:44.760
a dear friend and writer
who I deeply respect timidly

70
00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:48.480
made a confession after hearing parts
of this craft talk about my work.

71
00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.600
They were slated to review my first book,
But A Storm Is Blowing

72
00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.600
from Paradise
when it was published in 2012.

73
00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.800
The review never happened,
they admitted, because after reading

74
00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:00.960
the book, they realized
they did not know how to talk about it.

75
00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.960
They had no quote frame of reference
for it.

76
00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:07.000
In retrospect, they could have
just called me up and asked.

77
00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.280
But now, looking through the arc
that I laid out,

78
00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:10.680
it made more sense to them,

79
00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:14.000
perhaps because I built its reference
points throughout my later work.

80
00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:18.240
But first, the anecdote that leads
to the question and some ideas that

81
00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:21.920
I'm considering as a writer with a wide
ranging and experimental practice.

82
00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:24.960
This year, I attended the 2026

83
00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:28.320
Association of Writers
and Writing Programs annual conference.

84
00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:30.320
Think analog. Yellow.

85
00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:34.160
Multiplied by 100 or 4000.

86
00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:38.920
I usually skip this convening
out of cynicism for the whole supercharged

87
00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:43.280
affair, but this year, the MFA program
that I direct was a conference sponsor,

88
00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.080
and it took place only a couple miles
from my house, so I had to go.

89
00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:51.320
The sponsorship afforded the program
a prime booth location at the front of the

90
00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:55.600
conference book fair, and the book fair
is the real highlight of the conference.

91
00:04:55.880 --> 00:05:01.320
And in recent years, at conferences
like AWP, MLA, HRO, and other university

92
00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.120
symposia and conferences,

93
00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:06.400
there's been an increase in panels
and talks about artificial intelligence.

94
00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:08.800
I'm sure you've noticed this too.

95
00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:11.560
While most of these use
the phrasing AI as the catch

96
00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:15.120
all term aligned with popular conceptions
among laypersons,

97
00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:19.440
what most actually talk about
are large language models or llms.

98
00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.560
I'm reasonably confident
that I don't need to define

99
00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.040
AI or llms for this audience,
so I'll skip over that.

100
00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:31.560
And as an interested party, a writer who
has experimented with llms pre ChatGPT.

101
00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:34.640
I looked forward to the panel
organized by the Authors Guild

102
00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:38.640
and moderated by writer friend
Balcony Vara called Writers Resisting

103
00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:41.880
AI despite their use of the catch
all term.

104
00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:45.720
I knew they would be talking about Llms,
and I also had some idea

105
00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:47.640
as to the posture of the panelists.

106
00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:50.920
Given the blanket term,
AI was attached to resist.

107
00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:54.880
If this linkage was designed
to pull in an audience, it worked.

108
00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:57.520
The ballroom was standing room
only, packed

109
00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:01.320
with an almost evangelical
resistance among attendees.

110
00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:05.360
I was so in the air you could feel it.

111
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:09.080
One of the panelists was Alex
Hannah, coauthor

112
00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:12.280
with Emily Bender of the excellent book
The AI Khan.

113
00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:15.640
In it, Bender and Hannah offer
examples of everyday

114
00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:19.440
individual acts of AI resistance,
such as simply not using

115
00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:22.960
AI tools, turning off AI options,
and so on.

116
00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:28.520
They also promote what Hannah in the panel
called ridicule as praxis.

117
00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:32.920
In the book it is make fun of it
and troll the hell out of them.

118
00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:37.080
Ridiculous practice was then
enthusiastically championed by another

119
00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:40.640
panelist, highly regarded writer Carmen
Maria machado.

120
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:45.440
If a writer admits to using AI
by which they really mean llms,

121
00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:51.000
or perhaps has even speculated of doing
so, ridicule them, shame them, and cancel

122
00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:54.400
their contracts, as recently happened
with the book Shy Girl.

123
00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:58.280
What the panelists didn't know
was that they had just given me

124
00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:03.200
one way of interpreting an encounter
I had that very morning at 905.

125
00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:06.160
As soon as the book fair opened,
I was approached by a woman

126
00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:09.160
making a beeline for the booth
I was standing at.

127
00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:13.360
She approached my university program table
and opened the conversation by stating

128
00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:15.800
how much respect she had
for the institution

129
00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:19.080
that is the University of Maryland
and his MFA program.

130
00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:23.080
She held this respect right up
until she read my faculty bio

131
00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:27.320
on the program website, and saw that
I had written a book with AI, but

132
00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:32.920
she took to mean that I must be teaching
students to right use AI to write for her.

133
00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.120
One necessarily must lead to the other.

134
00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:37.200
She demanded to

135
00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:41.160
know what I was doing
after seeing this in my bio.

136
00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:45.000
She said she instantly lost all respect
for the program, and the school

137
00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:48.720
clicked away and posted a nasty comment
on social media.

138
00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:50.840
I didn't ask which one.

139
00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:54.400
Shame on me was the message
for teaching students to write with AI.

140
00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:59.160
I don't, by the way, in the panel
they did say to punch up

141
00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:03.040
when doing ridicule not down,
which I interpret as not targeting

142
00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:07.840
individuals so much as companies, brands,
institutions, organizations, etc.

143
00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:12.760
as a sidebar, one can see how quickly
claims of AI can be weaponized

144
00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:13.560
against already

145
00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:17.640
marginalized writers by other writers
with real or imagined grievances.

146
00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:22.760
Accusation can flow in the same direction
as power, which is why who is accusing?

147
00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:25.760
Being accused may not be incidental.

148
00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:28.400
But I do want to be fair to Bender
and Hannah's.

149
00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:31.400
Our larger argument,
most of which I agree with

150
00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:34.440
as a tactic
aimed upward at companies in AI hype.

151
00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:37.200
It's a legitimate tactic
for legitimate target.

152
00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:39.200
The trouble is, category drift.

153
00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:43.200
When a tactic designed for corporations
doesn't stay aimed at corporations.

154
00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:47.120
We can see how it can drift onto
individual writers and experiments,

155
00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:51.000
and stops being about labor politics
and becomes more about esthetic or process

156
00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:52.720
policing, i.e.

157
00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:54.520
I feel your process is a legitimate.

158
00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:56.960
Therefore, so is the work.

159
00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:59.360
So just what do I think I am doing?

160
00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:00.920
I have spent most of my writing life

161
00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:03.920
being asked versions of this question,
often implicitly.

162
00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:07.720
As a queer nonbinary person of color,
most of my personal life

163
00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:09.680
has been spent
answering similar questions.

164
00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:14.080
And so this type of spontaneous tribunal
has been more of a feature than a bug.

165
00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:18.680
As a writer, I have resisted explaining
or over explaining my own work

166
00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:22.840
out of a reluctance to commit the details
of an ever changing practice to paper.

167
00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:26.200
But this question,
as it applies to writing, isn't new.

168
00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:29.120
It's an old question
ask of experimental writers

169
00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:32.120
and writing and a practitioners
of electronic literature.

170
00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:36.720
Let me return to Russell for a moment,
lest you become your decoration.

171
00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:41.720
His work was odd, unclassifiable, and
received with indifference or derision.

172
00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:43.880
He struggled to find his public.

173
00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:48.280
In 1911 1912,
he staged a theatrical production

174
00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:51.280
of his novel Impressions of Africa,
to which the audience

175
00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:54.720
is reported to have jeered and booed
a form of ridicule.

176
00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:58.080
Yet in the audience
offering appreciation were Marcel

177
00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:01.080
Duchamp, Apollinaire, and Francis Picabia.

178
00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:05.800
Later admirers and defenders
included André Breton and Paul Éluard.

179
00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:08.840
He was admired by the Surrealists,
and his mechanistic

180
00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:11.840
style of composition
has been said to be proto,

181
00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:16.080
and his work was later
rediscovered in the 1950s by the Oulipo.

182
00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:19.640
While some recall, others do the reading,

183
00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:23.720
Roussel answered the question as to
what he was doing from beyond the grave.

184
00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:26.840
In the posthumous book
from which this talks title is taken,

185
00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:30.320
Roussel may be more patterned than Edge
Case.

186
00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:31.200
Gertrude Stein,

187
00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:35.240
John Cage, Jackson Pollock,
and the Aleppo were dismissed as nonsense.

188
00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:39.000
Not writing, not music,
not composition or plain parlor games.

189
00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:43.280
Procedural practices of the past 100 years
have been met with similar demands,

190
00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:46.400
accusations, questions, and yes, ridicule

191
00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:50.080
is a computationally generated poem,
even a poem.

192
00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:50.480
Is it?

193
00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:53.240
Creativity is what you're doing
and how you're doing it.

194
00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:56.920
Legible as writing,
let alone worth being called literature.

195
00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:00.960
Note that ridiculous practice requires
no knowledge whatsoever

196
00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:02.840
of the thing being ridiculed.

197
00:11:02.840 --> 00:11:04.720
Ridicule, especially uninformed

198
00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:08.320
ridicule, like a snap judgment,
is structured to prevent knowledge.

199
00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:09.840
The moment you understand

200
00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:13.440
a process of procedure,
it becomes harder to call it nonsense.

201
00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:17.440
Doing the reading forfeits the pleasure
of outright dismissing the book,

202
00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:21.240
and there is unearned pleasure and power
in the dismissal of the work of others.

203
00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:25.280
But again, sometimes
difficulty is a failure of the writer.

204
00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:29.600
I won't pretend it isn't, but other times
it is a condition of the work.

205
00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:32.680
And I think of my friend and poet Douglas
Kearney.

206
00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:34.920
Famously difficult.

207
00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:36.720
His poems aren't digital poems,

208
00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:39.720
but they wouldn't be possible
without digital tools.

209
00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:44.560
Normative reading treats the difficult,
legible or unpredicted the same way

210
00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:49.000
any normative system would as an error
or problem that needs to be collapsed

211
00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:53.400
back into the nearest recognizable shape,
often a representative one.

212
00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:57.440
I would argue that the writers I descend
from, including Kearney, and that some of

213
00:11:57.440 --> 00:12:01.480
you may also descend from refuse
this type of collapse in norming.

214
00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:04.920
We prefer a permutation space
that amplifies what a poem

215
00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:07.920
or text can be, and how it can be made.

216
00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:11.240
For me, this insistence emerges
from a core interest

217
00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:15.560
that carries into my creative practice
that of systems interrogation,

218
00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:20.280
going inside a system and using its logics
as the instruments of its explanation.

219
00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:24.720
But I'm getting ahead of myself
into the argument before the explanation,

220
00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:28.160
and before the language models
that led me to large language models.

221
00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:32.960
As a young writer, I initially invested
a great deal in language's ability

222
00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:36.400
to represent an express,
mostly mostly through metaphor.

223
00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:38.280
Did this feel possible?

224
00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:41.000
Over time, languages
limitations became apparent

225
00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:44.600
when I tried and failed
at so-called accurate representation.

226
00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:46.720
That's
because language is always symbolic.

227
00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:48.760
One step removed, a just adequate

228
00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:52.840
enough substitute for the real thing
that is embodied experience.

229
00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:56.680
And as I pushed further into limitations,
I found myself drawn to the outer

230
00:12:56.680 --> 00:13:01.280
edges of language to where can fracture
and buckle, be broken down and recombined.

231
00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:04.320
But being drawn to the outer
edges of language

232
