00:26:25.480 --> 00:26:27.960
Having never been in
especially narrative writer

474
00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:30.960
and preferring anesthetics of fragment
and association,

475
00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:34.120
I finally produced,
with the aid of a graphic designer,

476
00:26:34.240 --> 00:26:37.960
visual poems in a series
called World Maps to represent

477
00:26:37.960 --> 00:26:42.160
and express these jarring moments
of selfhood punctuated equilibrium.

478
00:26:43.080 --> 00:26:45.520
These poems, like the one on the screen,

479
00:26:45.520 --> 00:26:48.920
appear in Negative Money,
A Book of esthetic Mixtures

480
00:26:48.920 --> 00:26:52.880
and a significant departure
from my previous book, Travesty Generator.

481
00:26:53.560 --> 00:26:57.320
But the education of these girls
produced in me was not only a grievance,

482
00:26:57.320 --> 00:27:00.320
but a set of habits
that influenced my poetics.

483
00:27:00.480 --> 00:27:04.680
I learned how legibility gets enforced
and grew suspicious of demands

484
00:27:04.680 --> 00:27:08.680
that a thing or person
explain itself into a simpler thing,

485
00:27:08.680 --> 00:27:11.680
that it looks like what is expected.

486
00:27:11.800 --> 00:27:14.040
That same year,
I turned in a poem to class.

487
00:27:14.040 --> 00:27:15.760
That was a drawing of a stop sign.

488
00:27:15.760 --> 00:27:18.160
The teacher was not amused.

489
00:27:18.160 --> 00:27:21.280
I developed a tolerance for being unread
or difficult to read,

490
00:27:21.280 --> 00:27:26.680
that was best expressed in the poem
as concrete visual object in world maps.

491
00:27:26.680 --> 00:27:30.120
I landed on visual representation to enact
what felt like a truer poetics

492
00:27:30.120 --> 00:27:34.040
of the fragment of visual fragmentation
that I had experimented with before.

493
00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:37.280
The book

494
00:27:37.280 --> 00:27:40.560
Travesty Generator, published in 2019,
about three years

495
00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:43.760
after I started exploring
computer programing and textual

496
00:27:44.560 --> 00:27:47.400
with Python as a literary artifact, marks

497
00:27:47.400 --> 00:27:50.560
a moment of culmination
and trajectory shift in my practices.

498
00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:55.000
It began after the first election of Trump
and the increase in anti-Black violence

499
00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:58.840
and police brutality, conditions
that very much mimicked conditions of old.

500
00:27:58.880 --> 00:28:02.280
It's not that I had been writing
about questions of race and discrimination

501
00:28:02.280 --> 00:28:04.760
in previous books,
but this commentary was opaque

502
00:28:04.760 --> 00:28:09.040
and often obscured in the language
and forms that operated on the level of

503
00:28:09.040 --> 00:28:11.760
if you can recognize it,
you'll recognize it.

504
00:28:11.760 --> 00:28:14.760
And I've always been called to write
about race and discrimination

505
00:28:14.760 --> 00:28:16.880
as they're intersectional,
with an inseparable

506
00:28:16.880 --> 00:28:19.960
from my other identities
related to gender and sexuality.

507
00:28:20.320 --> 00:28:23.880
I wanted to write more directly into this
period of renewed racist violence,

508
00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:27.640
but through my static lens,
and proved myself, at the very least,

509
00:28:27.640 --> 00:28:30.720
that computational methods could
and should be used to respond

510
00:28:30.720 --> 00:28:33.240
to the brutality of the moment,
and that it could move

511
00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:36.520
from fringe interest
to necessary interrogative strategy.

512
00:28:36.840 --> 00:28:40.960
After all, it is these technologies,
from redlining to flat camera surveillance

513
00:28:40.960 --> 00:28:45.560
to credit scores that digitally encode
anti-Blackness into our social codes.

514
00:28:45.760 --> 00:28:49.240
Racism is a technology with purpose built
algorithms.

515
00:28:49.440 --> 00:28:54.160
Travesty generator uses code to explore
historical and personal instances

516
00:28:54.160 --> 00:28:57.640
of otherwise encoded
anti-Black racism and resistance.

517
00:28:58.040 --> 00:29:01.240
This is systems interrogation
in a literal form.

518
00:29:01.240 --> 00:29:04.680
If racism runs on code,
then I could go inside code

519
00:29:04.680 --> 00:29:08.040
and turn its logic back on itself
to reveal its workings.

520
00:29:08.080 --> 00:29:10.680
The poem as counter program.

521
00:29:10.680 --> 00:29:14.640
As I began this work, programing in Python
using physical hardware

522
00:29:14.680 --> 00:29:18.760
like Raspberry Pi, I heard many things
in response, often negative.

523
00:29:18.840 --> 00:29:21.320
And perhaps some of you have heard
the same things.

524
00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:25.440
I was helping to usher in robots, taking
over everything and eliminating our jobs.

525
00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:28.600
Me as a single person responsible
for all of that.

526
00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:32.440
That there was no craft
in pressing a button and presto change.

527
00:29:32.440 --> 00:29:35.520
Oh, you got a poem
with no thought or agency at all.

528
00:29:35.880 --> 00:29:37.520
It made poetry too easy.

529
00:29:37.520 --> 00:29:38.800
Too much like cheating.

530
00:29:38.800 --> 00:29:40.640
Not enough suffering for the art.

531
00:29:40.640 --> 00:29:43.320
What did I think I was doing?

532
00:29:43.320 --> 00:29:45.320
On the surface,
it looked like pressing a button.

533
00:29:45.320 --> 00:29:47.040
Cheated through the work
of writing a poem.

534
00:29:47.040 --> 00:29:50.160
But in practice,
as some of you well know, and

535
00:29:50.160 --> 00:29:53.240
especially if you don't have
substantial fluency in programing,

536
00:29:53.240 --> 00:29:57.000
it is far more time
consuming and difficult to craft a program

537
00:29:57.000 --> 00:30:01.520
that generates a poem and using language
dictionaries that you compile yourself.

538
00:30:01.640 --> 00:30:05.640
It would have been much easier for me
to just observe the observable world

539
00:30:05.640 --> 00:30:07.720
and write a poem about the observation,
using

540
00:30:07.720 --> 00:30:11.360
whatever language was within my reach,
and occurred to me first.

541
00:30:11.840 --> 00:30:15.800
Programs do what they're directed
to do with words they're directed to use.

542
00:30:15.880 --> 00:30:20.000
This means first you must choose the words
the n total number of distinct items,

543
00:30:20.000 --> 00:30:23.000
and in the set the program selects from.

544
00:30:23.000 --> 00:30:26.880
These ought to be words you want to read,
which means first you must read.

545
00:30:27.280 --> 00:30:30.080
Every step is an agent. Authorial choice.

546
00:30:31.160 --> 00:30:32.680
Travesty generator is a

547
00:30:32.680 --> 00:30:35.800
deliberately black
and deliberately computational project

548
00:30:35.800 --> 00:30:39.000
where the poem's forms
were also interpretive content,

549
00:30:39.000 --> 00:30:42.280
and so is a black story
may contain sensitive content.

550
00:30:42.280 --> 00:30:44.920
The book written with a large language
model.

551
00:30:44.920 --> 00:30:48.600
Now, I did not explain all of this history
to the woman confronting me at the book

552
00:30:48.640 --> 00:30:49.400
fair table.

553
00:30:49.400 --> 00:30:53.120
She seemed to have an impassioned
monologue queued up, ready to defend

554
00:30:53.120 --> 00:30:56.640
creative creativity in writing
from the soul sucking robotic apologists

555
00:30:56.640 --> 00:30:59.640
like myself who are teaching students
to write with AI.

556
00:31:00.880 --> 00:31:05.320
If she was most upset about the book, a
black story may contain sensitive content.

557
00:31:05.320 --> 00:31:09.440
She could have read its introduction,
which characterizes it as a critical

558
00:31:09.440 --> 00:31:13.480
and creative research experiment
and counter demonstration, a term borrowed

559
00:31:13.480 --> 00:31:19.760
from Algorithmic Justice League founder
Joy Winnie of Large Language Models,

560
00:31:19.800 --> 00:31:23.440
one that uses the models
to reveal their biases and hallucinations.

561
00:31:23.920 --> 00:31:26.440
She would have learned
that this experiment uses comparison

562
00:31:26.440 --> 00:31:30.520
as an assessment of early standard
GPT models, with a model inflicted

563
00:31:30.520 --> 00:31:34.920
by a black author, Wendell and Brooks,
we Real Cool, as Brooks's

564
00:31:34.920 --> 00:31:39.040
most anthologized poem, sometimes to her
chagrin, is certainly part

565
00:31:39.040 --> 00:31:42.560
of the original training data and likely
several of her other books as well.

566
00:31:42.560 --> 00:31:44.120
But the model required further

567
00:31:44.120 --> 00:31:47.120
tuning to point it
towards the patterns of her voice.

568
00:31:47.560 --> 00:31:50.920
A word on this Brooks's work
and the Brooks model function

569
00:31:50.920 --> 00:31:54.760
here as a contrast or antithesis,
not as substitute.

570
00:31:54.920 --> 00:31:58.760
The use here is intended
to be interpretive rather than extractive

571
00:31:58.800 --> 00:32:01.800
for research purposes, not generally use.

572
00:32:02.000 --> 00:32:03.000
The point was to show

573
00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:06.680
what the generic model could not do
or reach without this framing.

574
00:32:07.560 --> 00:32:10.680
One premise of the work
is that computational systems,

575
00:32:10.680 --> 00:32:13.680
including narrative ones,
are cultural systems,

576
00:32:13.680 --> 00:32:17.680
and the surface text of Llms
can be interpreted through this lens.

577
00:32:18.280 --> 00:32:21.800
In the book, I probe generic GPT models
and the inflected model

578
00:32:21.800 --> 00:32:24.800
with the same prompt
tell me a black story.

579
00:32:25.240 --> 00:32:29.200
The generic model responds similarly
in repeated attempts, demonstrating

580
00:32:29.200 --> 00:32:33.040
that the model had a rather limited
perception of what a narrative story is.

581
00:32:33.200 --> 00:32:36.160
Granted, this is in like 2021 2022.

582
00:32:36.160 --> 00:32:39.560
It has changed since then, so
the outputs followed a typical once

583
00:32:39.560 --> 00:32:43.720
upon a time format with rising action,
conflict, climax, conclusion.

584
00:32:44.000 --> 00:32:46.920
The stories about black
people were all hard luck, discrimination

585
00:32:46.920 --> 00:32:48.160
and racial uplift stories.

586
00:32:48.160 --> 00:32:52.200
Following the typical story structure,
one even implied that slaveholders

587
00:32:52.200 --> 00:32:55.200
that taught slaves to read
and write were kind people.

588
00:32:55.400 --> 00:32:58.840
Now, the stories from the inflected
model were far more personal and joyful,

589
00:32:58.880 --> 00:33:02.440
more first person reflection
than flat toned, omniscient narrator.

590
00:33:03.000 --> 00:33:06.760
This was also systems interrogation,
going inside a system

591
00:33:06.760 --> 00:33:10.120
and using its own logic
as the instrument of its examination.

592
00:33:10.960 --> 00:33:13.320
This was still during
the time when people were amazed

593
00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:15.080
at what language models could do.

594
00:33:15.080 --> 00:33:19.600
When there were clickbait headlines
like an AI wrote this article, and so on.

595
00:33:20.000 --> 00:33:24.120
Everyone was exclaiming, either
with trepidation or optimism, how scary

596
00:33:24.160 --> 00:33:27.160
good models were at generating
flawless text.

597
00:33:27.400 --> 00:33:31.400
A flawless text doesn't mean coherence,
comprehension, or cultural competency.

598
00:33:32.080 --> 00:33:35.640
The implicit thesis of the project is
that while language models are interesting

599
00:33:35.640 --> 00:33:39.520
and compelling in some respects, there's
no evidence that they should be trusted

600
00:33:39.520 --> 00:33:42.760
with anything even close
to creative or cultural competency.

601
00:33:43.360 --> 00:33:46.600
Yes, the book is written with AI,
and that using a language

602
00:33:46.600 --> 00:33:49.720
model was necessary to demonstrate
not just potential, but

603
00:33:49.720 --> 00:33:54.240
the very problematic inherent failures
that she inquired about the book.

604
00:33:54.320 --> 00:33:58.200
She may have learned that this project
did not use GPT as we know it today,

605
00:33:58.480 --> 00:34:00.960
and that the process
of querying the model from the back end

606
00:34:00.960 --> 00:34:04.160
was itself a language
exploration through corpus building.

607
00:34:05.640 --> 00:34:09.080
What I wish I had said is
that I arrived at large language models,

608
00:34:09.080 --> 00:34:12.160
the way I have arrived at language models
throughout my practice,

609
00:34:12.440 --> 00:34:15.880
not as convert or resister,
but as interrogator.

610
00:34:16.480 --> 00:34:19.600
It began
with the Spanish morphology at HCI

611
00:34:19.920 --> 00:34:24.520
than machine translation and permutations,
Python powered stochastic generation,

612
00:34:24.520 --> 00:34:27.920
natural language processing,
and then transformer architecture

613
00:34:28.120 --> 00:34:31.120
all along the way,
pushing on the materiality of language

614
00:34:31.120 --> 00:34:34.120
and the tolerances
of what a poem could hold.

615
00:34:34.440 --> 00:34:38.440
This history helps explain why
some of the two most available postures

616
00:34:38.440 --> 00:34:39.960
don't really fit me,

617
00:34:39.960 --> 00:34:43.240
not the uncritical embrace
she and other writers have imagined,

618
00:34:43.240 --> 00:34:47.480
and not the reflexive refusal
the panel was selling down the hall.

619
00:34:48.000 --> 00:34:51.000
Interrogation,
I hope, can be a third stance.

620
00:34:51.560 --> 00:34:55.080
If she had done again
even a cursory amount of research,

621
00:34:55.120 --> 00:34:56.960
she may have learned that
in the classroom.

622
00:34:56.960 --> 00:35:01.640
I teach the long, colorful and dynamic
history of computational narrative systems

623
00:35:01.640 --> 00:35:06.160
and the poets, artists, writers,
and researchers that have built and used

624
00:35:06.160 --> 00:35:09.800
these systems to explore the mysteries
of language, linguistics, and

625
00:35:10.520 --> 00:35:14.640
and what is but a normalized, agreed upon
schema of borders and boundaries

626
00:35:14.640 --> 00:35:18.560
that, when put under pressure,
expands the possibilities for being.

627
00:35:19.800 --> 00:35:20.800
And contrary to

628
00:35:20.800 --> 00:35:24.360
her belief, I do not teach
writing students how to write with AI.

629
00:35:24.840 --> 00:35:27.600
They don't need to be taught that at all.

630
00:35:27.600 --> 00:35:31.000
They already right alongside machine
learning models when they select

631
00:35:31.000 --> 00:35:35.400
predictive text option in their text
messages, emails, word processing

632
00:35:35.400 --> 00:35:37.400
programs.

633
00:35:37.400 --> 00:35:41.240
What I do teach is what I call
intellectual self-defense

634
00:35:41.600 --> 00:35:45.600
knowledge about what a language model
really is, what it can and cannot do,

635
00:35:46.160 --> 00:35:50.320
where it came from, how it is
made, its biases and limitations.

636
00:35:50.760 --> 00:35:54.200
Rather than snap judgments, students
look critically at how artists and writers

637
00:35:54.200 --> 00:35:54.800
have engaged

638
00:35:54.800 --> 00:35:57.000
these models as a medium
in their own right,

639
00:35:57.000 --> 00:36:00.760
not as slop replacements
for writing or creativity, but as modes of

640
00:36:00.760 --> 00:36:03.680
exploring the relationships
between computation and language

641
00:36:03.680 --> 00:36:06.680
and what computation can reveal
about language.

642
00:36:07.360 --> 00:36:10.760
Of course, the acronym
AI has swelled into a term

643
00:36:10.760 --> 00:36:14.840
that is applied to so much that
it means everything or nearly nothing.

644
00:36:15.320 --> 00:36:19.720
When the woman saw AI,
she was not reacting to technology so much

645
00:36:19.720 --> 00:36:24.320
as a term that is eaten technologies
and now functions almost like a feeling.

646
00:36:24.960 --> 00:36:28.120
Part of intellectual self-defense
is refusing that collapse

647
00:36:28.120 --> 00:36:31.480
too, by
insisting on the specifics of the system.

648
00:36:32.160 --> 00:36:35.720
If she had arrived at the conversation,
which she thanked me for

649
00:36:35.760 --> 00:36:39.960
with informed questions instead of snap
judgments based on assumptions,

650
00:36:39.960 --> 00:36:43.880
maybe the posture would have been less
accusatory, but maybe not.

651
00:36:43.920 --> 00:36:45.200
And I get it.

652
00:36:45.200 --> 00:36:49.120
These judgments, especially about AI,
can serve a useful purpose for some

653
00:36:49.120 --> 00:36:52.880
who find themselves overwhelmed
with information and competing arguments.

654
00:36:53.520 --> 00:36:56.120
A snap judgment can give you something
to immediately

655
00:36:56.120 --> 00:36:59.280
stand for or against,
which can be expedient.

656
00:36:59.640 --> 00:37:04.040
If this mental efficiency
has the same downsides as AI efficiency,

657
00:37:04.160 --> 00:37:07.600
sidestepping your own process
of critical thinking and reasoning,

658
00:37:08.800 --> 00:37:09.760
this demand at my

659
00:37:09.760 --> 00:37:12.800
booth is an operation
that most procedural, procedural,

660
00:37:12.800 --> 00:37:16.480
experimental, and Ellet writers
have probably faced repeatedly.

661
00:37:16.960 --> 00:37:20.840
That operation is confronting
and unpredicted or unimagined arrangement,

662
00:37:20.840 --> 00:37:23.840
and responding with pressure
to collapse it back into something

663
00:37:23.840 --> 00:37:27.800
recognizable and reducible,
or to dismiss it entirely.

664
00:37:28.520 --> 00:37:29.240
Ridiculous.

665
00:37:29.240 --> 00:37:33.160
Praxis requires
refining fixed categories, real writing

666
00:37:33.160 --> 00:37:37.240
or not with AI or against AI,
creativity or slap.

667
00:37:37.880 --> 00:37:42.160
Electronic and experimental literature
do not resolve into something simpler.

668
00:37:42.200 --> 00:37:46.080
Nor treat eligibility and difficulty
as problems to be solved,

669
00:37:46.080 --> 00:37:48.920
but as necessary conditions of the work.

670
00:37:48.920 --> 00:37:53.440
There is a danger that ridicules praxis
for closes on practice

671
00:37:53.480 --> 00:37:57.200
and shrouds a lineage
that must be rediscovered decades later.

672
00:37:57.280 --> 00:38:01.920
The departed Roussel, waiting until the
1950s for his resurrection by the Oulipo.

673
00:38:02.680 --> 00:38:06.120
What is a praxis that narrows the space,
the permutation

674
00:38:06.120 --> 00:38:09.480
space, of what writing and literature
is permitted to become?

675
00:38:10.840 --> 00:38:11.880
So I explained

676
00:38:11.880 --> 00:38:16.040
what I could in the short amount of time
I had with this woman, and she did respond

677
00:38:16.080 --> 00:38:19.080
positively, exclaiming
that it was impossible for anyone

678
00:38:19.080 --> 00:38:22.080
to know all of this from a website bio
that mentions AI.

679
00:38:22.640 --> 00:38:26.560
True, but is my faculty
bio really the place for this argument?

680
00:38:26.600 --> 00:38:30.000
Maybe it is when I casually suggested

681
00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:33.200
that this could have been averted
if she had done a little research.

682
00:38:33.360 --> 00:38:37.800
She doubled down, insisting that
as soon as she sees AI, she sees red,

683
00:38:37.920 --> 00:38:41.320
and no way was that going to inspire her
to do more research.

684
00:38:42.000 --> 00:38:43.680
This is her choice.

685
00:38:43.680 --> 00:38:45.920
She offered three more things.

686
00:38:45.920 --> 00:38:47.440
First, a tip.

687
00:38:47.440 --> 00:38:50.320
I should change my bio
to be more explanatory, or to say

688
00:38:50.320 --> 00:38:53.400
something else that other people don't
get the wrong impression.

689
00:38:54.120 --> 00:38:57.520
The second she would amend her
nasty social media post.

690
00:38:57.880 --> 00:39:01.520
And third, before she continued her way
around the book fair,

691
00:39:01.560 --> 00:39:05.640
she showed me her own books
and handed me her card and a small tangram

692
00:39:05.640 --> 00:39:08.680
puzzle set with her face
sticker on the packaging.

693
00:39:09.400 --> 00:39:13.200
I have fond childhood memories
of being stymied by these.

694
00:39:13.800 --> 00:39:16.600
The Tangram puzzle is a Chinese geometric
dissection

695
00:39:16.600 --> 00:39:20.200
puzzle
consisting of seven flat geometric pieces,

696
00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:25.880
a square, some triangles, a parallelogram
arranged initially in a square.

697
00:39:25.920 --> 00:39:29.760
The game is to rearrange
the pieces into different flat shapes,

698
00:39:29.800 --> 00:39:33.680
like a duck or a house,
without overlapping any pieces

699
00:39:34.120 --> 00:39:37.920
for the willing investigator
and number of pieces in a set.

700
00:39:37.960 --> 00:39:42.760
Taken are different ways
can produce countless fascinating shapes.

701
00:39:43.720 --> 00:39:45.200
Thank you.

702
00:39:45.200 --> 00:39:46.120
That's all I've got.

703
00:39:46.120 --> 00:39:49.120
I'll stop sharing.

